Chapter 2: Theoretical Foundations and Central Hypothesis

2.1 Civilizational Adaptive Capacity: A Theoretical Construct

Define civilizational adaptive capacity at time  t , denoted  \Omega(t) , as:

\Omega(t) = \Pr\{\text{civilization maintains or restores essential functions following perturbation at time } t\} \times \Pr\{\text{option space for future flourishing is preserved}\}

This definition decomposes adaptive capacity into two components: functional resilience (the probability of maintaining or restoring essential functions) and option-space preservation (the probability that the perturbation does not irreversibly foreclose desirable future trajectories). This decomposition is theoretically motivated: a civilization that survives a catastrophe but emerges with permanently reduced option space (e.g., a totalitarian surveillance state) is not "adaptive" in the full sense.

The construct is multidimensional, encompassing technological redundancy, institutional responsiveness, cognitive diversity, and infrastructural modularity. The relative importance of these dimensions is an open empirical question that the framework proposes to address through the measurement program in Chapter 5.

2.2 Central Hypothesis H1

H1: Civilizational adaptive capacity is constrained primarily by the correlation structure of failure modes across critical systems, rather than by the mean capability of individual systems.

Formally, let  \mathbf{X} = (X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n)  be the vector of failure indicators for  n  critical systems, where  X_i = 1  denotes failure of system  i . Define:

\mu_i = \Pr(X_i = 1) \quad \text{(mean failure probability)}

\Sigma = \text{Corr}(\mathbf{X}) \quad \text{(correlation matrix of failure indicators)}

Then H1 asserts:

\frac{\partial \Omega}{\partial \|\Sigma\|} > \frac{\partial \Omega}{\partial \|\boldsymbol{\mu}\|} \quad \text{for relevant variations in } \Sigma \text{ and } \boldsymbol{\mu}

That is, small reductions in correlation produce larger gains in adaptive capacity than small reductions in mean failure probability. This is a quantitative, falsifiable claim.

H1 decomposes into three independently testable sub-hypotheses:

H1a: Adding more technological layers without reducing inter-system correlation produces diminishing or negative returns in adaptive capacity.

H1b: Institutional innovations that reduce coupling between critical systems increase  \Omega(t)  even when they do not increase the capability of any individual system.

H1c: The relationship between technological complexity and civilizational resilience is non-monotonic: beyond a threshold, additional complexity without corresponding coordination capacity reduces resilience.

For H1c, we adopt the formal structure from interdependent networks theory. Let  p  be a coupling parameter (fraction of interdependent nodes) and  p_c  the critical threshold. H1c states:

\Omega(p) \text{ is continuous for } p < p_c \quad \text{(graceful degradation)}

\Omega(p) \text{ exhibits a discontinuity at } p = p_c \quad \text{(first-order transition)}

\Omega(p) = 0 \text{ for } p > p_c \quad \text{(catastrophic collapse)}

The shape is a cliff, not a hill. This characterization is drawn from Buldyrev et al. (2010) with finite-size corrections from Radicchi (2015).

2.3 Methodological Commitments

Eight commitments govern all formal and empirical work proposed in this framework:

Commitment 1: No decorative equations. Every formal expression must have operational definitions for its variables, specified measurement protocols, and stated uncertainty bounds. Equations that cannot meet this standard are removed.

Commitment 2: Engagement with existing literature. Proposals must cite and engage with existing research programs, including those that contradict the framework.

Commitment 3: Political economy as a first-class constraint. Every innovation is analyzed in terms of who controls it, who benefits, who is exposed to its failure modes, and what incentives shape its deployment.

Commitment 4: Distinguish technical readiness from civilizational importance.

Commitment 5: Falsifiability over comprehensiveness. Proposals must specify what observations would disconfirm them.

Commitment 6: Tail-risk adequacy. Formal models of catastrophic failure must be capable of representing joint extreme events. Models with zero tail dependence are rejected on structural grounds.

Commitment 7: Actor specification. Claims about what "should" be done must identify plausible actors, their incentives, and adoption mechanisms.

Commitment 8: Normative explicitness. Value commitments are stated explicitly rather than smuggled in through technical vocabulary.

These commitments shape the formal development in Chapters 3 through 8.

2.4 Relationship to Existing Literature

This framework integrates four research traditions:

Cliodynamics (Turchin, 2016; Tainter, 1988; Seshat Databank) provides empirical regularities about political instability and collapse. The framework engages with these regularities as potential constraints on theoretical models but does not conduct original cliodynamic analysis.

Interdependent network theory (Buldyrev, Parshani, Paul, Stanley, & Havlin, 2010; Radicchi, 2015) provides formal structure for abrupt, system-wide failure. The framework adopts this formalism and extends it to heterogeneous, finite networks relevant to real infrastructure.

Common-pool resource governance (Ostrom, 1990; Crawford & Ostrom, 1995) identifies design principles for successful collective action without centralized authority. The framework integrates these principles into its measurement program.

Composite indicator methodology (Saltelli, 2007; Munda, 2008; Saisana, Saltelli, & Tarantola, 2005) exposes the pitfalls of aggregating heterogeneous dimensions into a single index. The framework adopts the non-compensatory critique and builds measurement protocols accordingly.

The integration of these traditions yields a theoretical framework that is internally coherent and ready for empirical specification.

2.5 Epistemic Status

This dissertation follows the classification of theory contributions articulated by Whetten (1989): the contribution is in the what (constructs), how (relationships), and why (causal mechanisms) of civilizational adaptive capacity, formulated with sufficient precision to enable future empirical testing. The framework should be evaluated on whether it is falsifiable, operational, and theoretically generative—not on whether it has been empirically confirmed.

