Chapter 7: Normative Commitments and Value Framework

7.1 Option Space as Instrumental Value

Option space—the ability to choose among futures—is valuable because it enables adaptation to unforeseen circumstances. It is instrumental, not terminal. Some futures require foreclosing others; the choice of which options to exercise is a normative question that cannot be resolved by technical analysis.

Formally, define option space  \mathcal{O}(t)  as the set of feasible future trajectories:

\mathcal{O}(t) = \{ \text{trajectories } \tau : \tau \text{ is achievable from state at time } t \}

The instrumental value of option space is  V(\mathcal{O}) = \max_{\tau \in \mathcal{O}} U(\tau)  for some utility function  U . This formulation makes clear that option space is valuable because it enables optimization over a larger set of possibilities, not because options are valuable in themselves.

7.2 Flourishing as Terminal Value

The terminal value is the flourishing of intelligent life, including but not limited to human life, under conditions of self-determination. "Flourishing" is deliberately left open to specification through democratic deliberation, but this openness is not neutral: it encodes a commitment to non-domination and epistemic openness.

This commitment has implications for the measurement framework. If flourishing is the terminal value, then adaptive capacity  \Omega(t)  should be measured not merely as survival probability but as survival probability conditional on preserving the conditions for flourishing. This is why the definition in Section 2.1 includes option-space preservation as a component of adaptive capacity.

7.3 Non-Adaptable Losses and the Formal Link to Non-Compensatory Aggregation

Certain losses are not to be "adapted to":

1. Permanent extinction of intelligent life: no amount of post-hoc analysis or recovery can compensate.
2. Irreversible loss of civilizational option space: foreclosing futures that would have enabled flourishing.
3. Erosion of the conditions for democratic self-governance: undermining the very processes through which societies can deliberate about their future.

These are constraints, not variables to be optimized. The formal link to non-compensatory aggregation is direct:

In additive aggregation, weights have the meaning of substitution rates (Munda, 2008). If energy abundance has weight 0.8 and democratic self-governance has weight 0.2, then a loss of 1.0 in democratic self-governance can be compensated by a gain of 0.25 in energy abundance. This substitution is exactly what the non-adaptable losses constraint rejects.

In non-compensatory aggregation (geometric mean, outranking), no amount of improvement in one dimension can fully compensate for a critical loss in another dimension. The geometric mean is zero if any dimension is zero. Outranking methods produce incomparability rather than forced tradeoffs.

Formal statement:

\nexists \, (X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_{m-1}) \text{ such that } f(X_1, \ldots, X_{m-1}, 0) = f(X_1, \ldots, X_{m-1}, \epsilon) \text{ for } \epsilon > 0

In non-compensatory aggregation, there is no set of values for other dimensions that makes a zero in one dimension equivalent to a positive value in that dimension. This is the formal expression of the non-adaptable losses principle.

7.4 Procedural Legitimacy

Procedural legitimacy—decisions made through processes that those affected can recognize as fair—is a necessary condition for coordination capacity. This is grounded in Ostrom's (1990) empirical finding that participatory decision-making is a design principle for successful collective action.

The framework treats procedural legitimacy not as a luxury but as a binding constraint on coordination capacity. Institutions that achieve coordination through coercion or manipulation may temporarily increase  C_{\text{coordination}}  but do so at the cost of long-run stability and the option space that depends on self-determination.

7.5 Summary

The normative commitments are:

1. Option space is instrumentally valuable.
2. Flourishing under self-determination is the terminal value.
3. Non-adaptable losses cannot be compensated.
4. Procedural legitimacy is necessary for sustainable coordination.

These commitments are not empirically testable—they are value judgments. But they have immediate implications for the empirical program: they specify what counts as success (flourishing, not mere survival) and what constraints must not be violated (non-adaptable losses, procedural legitimacy).

