Chapter 8: Pre-Registered Falsification Infrastructure

8.1 Pre-Registered Hypotheses with Rejection Thresholds

Each hypothesis is specified with a dataset, method, and threshold for rejection. These are pre-registration criteria: they would be registered before data collection to prevent post-hoc interpretation.

H1a: Adding more technological layers without reducing inter-system correlation produces diminishing or negative returns in adaptive capacity.

· Dataset: STACK-style adversarial evaluation with two-layer classifier systems, one with shared training data and one with independent training data.
· Method: Compare catastrophic attack success rate (ASR) between shared-data and independent-data two-layer systems.
· Rejection threshold: H1a is rejected if the independent-data system does not reduce catastrophic ASR by at least 5 percentage points relative to the shared-data system, across 3 independent runs with different random seeds.
· Statistical criterion:  p < 0.05  using a permutation test with 10,000 permutations.

H1b: Institutional innovations that reduce coupling between critical systems increase  \Omega(t)  even when they do not increase the capability of any individual system.

· Dataset: Historical case studies of infrastructure decoupling programs (e.g., microgrid deployment, distributed storage adoption).
· Method: Difference-in-differences comparison of recovery time after disruption between coupled and decoupled regions.
· Rejection threshold: H1b is rejected if decoupled regions show no statistically significant improvement in recovery time (defined as time to restore 90% of pre-disruption functionality) compared to coupled regions.

H1c: The relationship between technological complexity and civilizational resilience is non-monotonic.

· Dataset: Two-layer infrastructure networks (power grid + communications network) with documented dependency links.
· Method: Finite-size percolation analysis using Radicchi decomposition to identify the transition type.
· Rejection threshold: H1c is rejected if the transition is second-order (continuous  S(p) ) rather than first-order (discontinuous  S(p) ) for the coupled system.

H3: Coordination capacity is domain-specific and cannot be captured by a single universal index.

· Dataset: ADICO-coded institutional arrangements across four domains (pandemic, nuclear, climate, AI governance).
· Method: Compare structural similarity of institutions across domains using cosine similarity of ADICO component vectors.
· Rejection threshold: H3 is rejected if cross-domain institutional similarity is high (cosine similarity > 0.8) and domain-specific variation explains less than 10% of total variance.

