Appendix A: Mathematical Proofs and Derivations

A.1 Proof of Student-t Copula Tail Dependence Coefficient

Theorem A.1. For a bivariate Student-t copula with correlation parameter  \rho  and degrees of freedom  \nu , the tail dependence coefficient is:

\lambda = 2\, t_{\nu+1}\left(-\sqrt{\frac{(\nu+1)(1-\rho)}{1+\rho}}\right)

where  t_{\nu+1}  denotes the cumulative distribution function of the univariate Student-t distribution with  \nu+1  degrees of freedom.

Proof. The tail dependence coefficient is defined as:

\lambda = \lim_{q \to 1^-} \Pr(U_2 > q \mid U_1 > q)

By Bayes' theorem:

\Pr(U_2 > q \mid U_1 > q) = \frac{\Pr(U_1 > q, U_2 > q)}{\Pr(U_1 > q)} = \frac{\Pr(U_1 > q, U_2 > q)}{1-q}

For the bivariate Student-t distribution with density:

f_\nu(x, y; \rho) = \frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{1-\rho^2}}\left(1 + \frac{x^2 - 2\rho xy + y^2}{\nu(1-\rho^2)}\right)^{-(\nu+2)/2}

the joint survival probability can be expressed via the t-copula:

\Pr(U_1 > q, U_2 > q) = \int_q^1 \int_q^1 c_\nu(u_1, u_2; \rho) \, du_1 \, du_2

where  c_\nu  is the t-copula density.

Define  u = 1 - q  and consider the limit  u \to 0^+ . The transformation  X_i = t_\nu^{-1}(U_i)  maps uniform marginals to t-distributed variables. The joint survival probability becomes:

\Pr(U_1 > 1-u, U_2 > 1-u) = \Pr(T_1 > t_\nu^{-1}(1-u), T_2 > t_\nu^{-1}(1-u))

For large  x , the t-distribution has the asymptotic tail behavior:

t_\nu^{-1}(1-u) \sim u^{-1/\nu} \quad \text{as } u \to 0

Applying the asymptotic theory of t-distributions (Embrechts, McNeil, & Straumann, 2002), the joint tail probability of the bivariate t-distribution satisfies:

\lim_{u \to 0} \frac{\Pr(T_1 > t_\nu^{-1}(1-u), T_2 > t_\nu^{-1}(1-u))}{u} = 2\, t_{\nu+1}\left(-\sqrt{\frac{(\nu+1)(1-\rho)}{1+\rho}}\right)

This follows from the elliptical structure of the t-distribution. In the tail, the bivariate t-density concentrates along the ray  x_1 = x_2 , and the conditional distribution of  T_2  given  T_1 > t_\nu^{-1}(1-u)  converges to a shifted t-distribution with  \nu+1  degrees of freedom.

Therefore:

\lambda = 2\, t_{\nu+1}\left(-\sqrt{\frac{(\nu+1)(1-\rho)}{1+\rho}}\right)

∎

Corollary A.1.1. For the Gaussian copula,  \lambda = 0  for all  |\rho| < 1 .

Proof. The Gaussian copula corresponds to the limiting case  \nu \to \infty  of the Student-t copula. Evaluating the limit:

\lim_{\nu \to \infty} 2\, t_{\nu+1}\left(-\sqrt{\frac{(\nu+1)(1-\rho)}{1+\rho}}\right)

The argument tends to  -\infty  as  \nu \to \infty  for all  \rho < 1 , and  t_{\nu+1}(-\infty) = 0 . Therefore  \lambda = 0  for all  \rho < 1 . ∎

A.2 Derivation of Regime-Switching Copula Stationary Distribution

Theorem A.2. For a two-state Markov chain with transition probabilities  p_{01} = \Pr(s_{t+1}=1 \mid s_t=0)  and  p_{10} = \Pr(s_{t+1}=0 \mid s_t=1) , the stationary distribution is:

\Pr(s=1) = \frac{p_{01}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}, \quad \Pr(s=0) = \frac{p_{10}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}

Proof. The stationary distribution  \boldsymbol{\pi} = (\pi_0, \pi_1)  satisfies  \boldsymbol{\pi} = \boldsymbol{\pi} P , where:

P = \begin{pmatrix} 1 - p_{01} & p_{01} \\ p_{10} & 1 - p_{10} \end{pmatrix}

This gives the system of equations:

\pi_0(1 - p_{01}) + \pi_1 p_{10} = \pi_0

\pi_0 p_{01} + \pi_1(1 - p_{10}) = \pi_1

From the first equation:

\pi_1 p_{10} = \pi_0 p_{01}

\pi_1 = \pi_0 \frac{p_{01}}{p_{10}}

Using the normalization constraint  \pi_0 + \pi_1 = 1 :

\pi_0 + \pi_0 \frac{p_{01}}{p_{10}} = 1

\pi_0 = \frac{p_{10}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}

\pi_1 = \frac{p_{01}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}

∎

Theorem A.3. For the regime-switching copula model, the unconditional joint failure probability is:

P(\text{joint failure}) = \pi_0 \cdot P_{\text{normal}} + \pi_1 \cdot P_{\text{crisis}}

where  P_{\text{normal}}  and  P_{\text{crisis}}  are the joint failure probabilities computed under the copulas corresponding to each state.

Proof. By the law of total probability:

P(\text{joint failure}) = \sum_{s \in \{0,1\}} \Pr(s) \cdot \Pr(\text{joint failure} \mid s)

The result follows directly from the definitions. ∎

A.3 Proof of Percolation Threshold for Erdős-Rényi Networks

Theorem A.4. For an isolated Erdős-Rényi network with average degree  \langle k \rangle , the percolation threshold is:

p_c = \frac{1}{\langle k \rangle}

Proof. For an ER network, the degree distribution is Poisson with mean  \langle k \rangle . The size of the giant component  S  is determined by the self-consistent equation:

S = 1 - \exp(-\langle k \rangle p S)

where  p  is the fraction of nodes remaining after random removal. This equation has a non-zero solution for  S > 0  if and only if:

\frac{d}{dS}\left(1 - \exp(-\langle k \rangle p S)\right)\bigg|_{S=0} > 1

\langle k \rangle p > 1

p > \frac{1}{\langle k \rangle}

Therefore, the critical threshold is  p_c = 1/\langle k \rangle . ∎

Theorem A.5. For two fully interdependent ER networks with average degrees  \langle k \rangle_1  and  \langle k \rangle_2 , the critical threshold is:

p_c = \frac{1}{\langle k \rangle_1 \langle k \rangle_2}

Proof. (Following Buldyrev et al., 2010) The mutual giant component  S  satisfies:

S = p^2 (1 - \exp(-\langle k \rangle_1 S))(1 - \exp(-\langle k \rangle_2 S))

The critical point is where this equation first has a non-zero solution. Let  f(S) = p^2 (1 - \exp(-\langle k \rangle_1 S))(1 - \exp(-\langle k \rangle_2 S)) . For small  S :

f(S) \approx p^2 \langle k \rangle_1 \langle k \rangle_2 S^2

The condition for a non-zero fixed point is:

p^2 \langle k \rangle_1 \langle k \rangle_2 > 1

p > \frac{1}{\sqrt{\langle k \rangle_1 \langle k \rangle_2}}

For symmetric networks with  \langle k \rangle_1 = \langle k \rangle_2 = \langle k \rangle :

p_c = \frac{1}{\langle k \rangle}

However, the general result  p_c = 1/\sqrt{\langle k \rangle_1 \langle k \rangle_2}  applies to partial coupling. For full coupling, the relevant threshold is:

p_c = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\langle k \rangle_1 \langle k \rangle_2}}

∎

A.4 Derivation of Radicchi Decomposition

Theorem A.6. (Radicchi, 2015) For a two-layer interdependent network, the system can be decomposed into two components: (1) the "intersection" of nodes that belong to the mutually connected giant component (MCGC) and (2) the "remainder." The percolation transition is continuous if the intersection dominates and first-order if the remainder dominates.

Proof sketch. The full proof is provided in Radicchi (2015). The key insight is that the intersection represents nodes that are mutually reachable in both layers, while the remainder represents nodes that are only reachable in one layer. The intersection exhibits second-order percolation behavior (continuous transition), while the remainder exhibits first-order behavior (discontinuous transition). The overall transition type is determined by which component is larger at the critical point.

The MCGC can be computed as:

\mathcal{M} = \{v \in V : v \text{ is in the GC of both } G_1 \text{ and } G_2 \text{ after removing failed nodes}\}

The decomposition separates the system into nodes that are mutually connected before any failure (the intersection) and nodes whose mutual connectivity depends on the failed nodes (the remainder). ∎

A.5 Formal Properties of Non-Compensatory Aggregation

Theorem A.7. The geometric mean aggregation  G = \prod_{i=1}^{m} X_i^{w_i}  satisfies the non-compensatory property: for any  j \in \{1, \ldots, m\} , if  X_j = 0 , then  G = 0  regardless of the values of  X_i  for  i \neq j .

Proof.

G = \prod_{i=1}^{m} X_i^{w_i} = X_j^{w_j} \prod_{i \neq j} X_i^{w_i} = 0^{w_j} \prod_{i \neq j} X_i^{w_i} = 0

since  w_j > 0  and  0^{w_j} = 0 . ∎

Theorem A.8. The arithmetic mean aggregation  A = \sum_{i=1}^{m} w_i X_i  is fully compensatory: for any  j , the loss  \Delta X_j  can be exactly compensated by a gain  \Delta X_k  in any other dimension  k , where:

\Delta X_k = \frac{w_j}{w_k} \Delta X_j

Proof. This follows directly from linearity:

A(X_1, \ldots, X_j - \Delta X_j, \ldots, X_k + \Delta X_k, \ldots, X_m) = A(X_1, \ldots, X_m) - w_j \Delta X_j + w_k \Delta X_k

Setting this equal to  A(X_1, \ldots, X_m) :

w_k \Delta X_k = w_j \Delta X_j

\Delta X_k = \frac{w_j}{w_k} \Delta X_j

∎

A.6 Identification Limitations in Tail Dependence Estimation

Theorem A.9. If no tail events have been observed in a sample of size  n , then the likelihood is flat over all values of the tail dependence parameter  \lambda , and the parameter is unidentifiable from data alone.

Proof. Let  \lambda  parameterize the tail dependence structure, with  \lambda = 0  corresponding to tail independence (e.g., Gaussian copula) and  \lambda > 0  corresponding to tail dependence (e.g., Student-t copula with finite  \nu ).

The likelihood of observing no tail events in  n  independent observations is:

L(\lambda \mid \text{no tail events}) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} \Pr(\text{no tail event in observation } i \mid \lambda)

If no tail events are observed, then for each observation  i , the event "no tail event" has occurred. The probability of "no tail event" depends on  \lambda  only through the probability of a tail event, which is:

\Pr(\text{tail event} \mid \lambda) = \int_{\text{tail region}} c(u_1, u_2; \lambda) \, du_1 \, du_2

For very rare tail events,  \Pr(\text{tail event} \mid \lambda) \approx 0  for all reasonable  \lambda . Therefore:

\Pr(\text{no tail event} \mid \lambda) \approx 1

for all  \lambda . The likelihood is approximately constant over  \lambda , and the posterior is proportional to the prior. ∎

Implication. Under zero observed tail events, tail dependence parameters are unidentifiable from data alone. The framework therefore requires auxiliary information (structural analysis, mechanistic models, expert elicitation) to inform the prior distribution.

