Appendix C: ADICO Coding Protocol and Complete Worked Examples

C.1 ADICO Coding Protocol

The following protocol operationalizes the ADICO coding framework (Crawford & Ostrom, 1995) for institutional analysis.

Step 1: Identify the institutional statement. Extract relevant rules from formal documents (treaties, regulations, organizational charters).

Step 2: Decompose into ADICO components:

· A (Attribute): Which actors does the rule apply to? Code as a category (e.g., "States Parties," "Member States," "Licensees").
· D (Deontic): What is the modal operator? Code as one of: MUST, MAY, MUST NOT.
· I (Aim): What is the prescribed action? Code as a verb phrase (e.g., "notify WHO," "pay contribution").
· C (Condition): Under what circumstances does the rule apply? Code as a condition phrase (e.g., "within 24 hours," "if vaccines are available").
· O (Or else): What is the sanction for non-compliance? Code as: FORMAL (specify mechanism), INFORMAL (reputational), or NONE.

Step 3: Assess structural completeness. A full rule has all five components. A norm lacks the "Or else" component. A shared strategy lacks both "Deontic" and "Or else."

Step 4: Compare across institutions. Use the structural profiles to identify systematic differences in institutional design across domains.

C.2 Complete ADICO Coding of Pandemic-Response Institutions

Institution 1: WHO International Health Regulations (IHR 2005), Article 6

Source text: "Each State Party shall assess events occurring within its territory... and shall notify WHO... of all events which may constitute a public health emergency of international concern... within 24 hours of assessment."

ADICO coding:

Component Code
A (Attribute) States Parties
D (Deontic) MUST
I (Aim) Notify WHO of events that may constitute a PHEIC
C (Condition) Within 24 hours of assessment
O (Or else) NONE (no formal sanction specified)

Structural classification: NORM (lacks formal sanction).

Institution 2: US CDC National Notifiable Diseases Surveillance System

Source text: "State health departments must report cases of certain infectious diseases to CDC. Reporting is a condition of receiving federal public health emergency preparedness funding."

ADICO coding:

Component Code
A (Attribute) State health departments
D (Deontic) MUST
I (Aim) Report cases of notifiable diseases to CDC
C (Condition) Within specified timeframes (varies by disease)
O (Or else) FORMAL: Loss of federal funding eligibility (partial)

Structural classification: FULL RULE (has formal sanction).

Institution 3: COVAX Facility (2020)

Source text: "Participating countries may access vaccine doses through pooled procurement. Countries that do not meet their financial commitments may not access subsequent allocations."

ADICO coding:

Component Code
A (Attribute) Participating countries
D (Deontic) MAY
I (Aim) Access vaccine doses through pooled procurement
C (Condition) If financial contribution paid and vaccines available
O (Or else) FORMAL: Inability to access subsequent allocations

Structural classification: FULL RULE (has embedded sanction).

C.3 Comparative Analysis

The three institutions exhibit systematically different structural properties:

Institution Deontic Sanction Type Structural Class
IHR 2005, Art. 6 MUST NONE NORM
CDC NNDSS MUST FORMAL (funding) FULL RULE
COVAX MAY FORMAL (exclusion) FULL RULE

The IHR 2005 lacks any formal sanction, making it a norm rather than a full rule. This structural feature helps explain the observed compliance problems: states have no material incentive to comply with notification requirements. The CDC and COVAX mechanisms embed sanctions, creating stronger compliance incentives.

This comparative analysis demonstrates the utility of ADICO coding for assessing coordination capacity without requiring a single scalar measure. The structural differences are qualitative and interpretable, providing a basis for domain-specific capacity assessment.

