Appendix D: Non-Compensatory Aggregation Methods

D.1 PROMETHEE Outranking Procedure

The following procedure implements PROMETHEE II for complete ranking (Brans & Mareschal, 2005). This is a theoretical description; no empirical application is claimed.

Step 1: Define alternatives and criteria. Let  \mathcal{A} = \{A_1, \ldots, A_n\}  be the set of alternatives (e.g., civilizations, institutions) and  \mathcal{G} = \{g_1, \ldots, g_m\}  the set of criteria with weights  w_1, \ldots, w_m .

Step 2: Calculate pairwise preference degrees. For each pair of alternatives  (A_i, A_k)  and each criterion  g_j :

P_j(A_i, A_k) = F_j\left(d_j(A_i, A_k)\right)

where  d_j(A_i, A_k) = g_j(A_i) - g_j(A_k)  and  F_j  is the preference function for criterion  j . Common choices include:

· Linear preference function with indifference and preference thresholds:

F_j(d) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } d \leq q_j \\
\frac{d - q_j}{p_j - q_j} & \text{if } q_j < d \leq p_j \\
1 & \text{if } d > p_j
\end{cases}

· Level preference function:

F_j(d) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } d \leq q_j \\
\frac{1}{2} & \text{if } q_j < d \leq p_j \\
1 & \text{if } d > p_j
\end{cases}

Step 3: Calculate weighted preference index. For each pair  (A_i, A_k) :

\pi(A_i, A_k) = \sum_{j=1}^{m} w_j \cdot P_j(A_i, A_k)

Step 4: Calculate outranking flows.

Positive outranking flow (strength of  A_i  over all others):

\phi^+(A_i) = \frac{1}{n-1} \sum_{A_k \in \mathcal{A}, k \neq i} \pi(A_i, A_k)

Negative outranking flow (weakness of  A_i  against all others):

\phi^-(A_i) = \frac{1}{n-1} \sum_{A_k \in \mathcal{A}, k \neq i} \pi(A_k, A_i)

Step 5: Calculate net outranking flow.

\phi(A_i) = \phi^+(A_i) - \phi^-(A_i)

Step 6: Rank alternatives by decreasing net outranking flow.

D.2 Illustrative Application to Three Hypothetical Civilizations

Consider three hypothetical civilizations scored on four dimensions:

Civilization Pandemic Prep Nuclear Safety Climate Mitigation AI Governance
A 0.7 0.6 0.5 0.4
B 0.3 0.8 0.7 0.6
C 0.1 0.9 0.9 0.8

Arithmetic mean (equal weights):

Civilization Arithmetic Mean
A 0.55
B 0.60
C 0.675

Geometric mean (equal weights):

Civilization Geometric Mean
A 0.54
B 0.56
C 0.49

The rankings differ because the geometric mean penalizes Civilization C for its very low pandemic preparedness (0.1), which cannot be compensated by its high scores in other dimensions. This demonstrates the non-compensatory property: under arithmetic aggregation, C ranks first; under geometric aggregation, A ranks first.

PROMETHEE II application (level preference function with  q = 0.1 ,  p = 0.3 , equal weights):

