Appendix H: Statement of Theoretical Status

This dissertation is a work of theory construction in the tradition of Type-1 theory development (Bacharach, 1989; Whetten, 1989). The contribution consists of:

1. Construct clarity: Formal definitions of civilizational adaptive capacity, coordination capacity, tail dependence, and related constructs with specified operational meaning.
2. Formally specified relationships: Mathematical characterizations of the relationships between variables (e.g., the correlation hypothesis H1, the non-monotonic complexity-resilience relationship H2).
3. Falsification criteria: Pre-registered rejection thresholds that specify what observations would disconfirm each hypothesis.
4. Measurement protocols: Operational procedures for measuring constructs (e.g., ADICO coding, non-compensatory aggregation, Radicchi decomposition).
5. Synthetic demonstrations: Computational illustrations of the formal properties of the proposed models, using theoretical parameter values rather than empirical data.

No empirical work has been conducted. The dissertation does not contain:

· Original data collection
· Analysis of real-world data
· Computational experiments on empirical datasets
· Physical tests or measurements
· Empirical validation of any hypothesis

All formal models, measurement protocols, and falsification criteria are presented as proposals for future empirical work. The synthetic demonstrations are theoretical calculations that explicate the formal properties of the models; they do not constitute empirical evidence.

The value of the dissertation lies in its contribution to theory construction: the framework is falsifiable, operationally specified, and addresses the actual binding constraints on civilizational resilience. The ultimate test lies in future empirical work that implements the proposed protocols and tests the proposed hypotheses.

